  "preamble": "The close-mid back rounded vowel [o] is realized as the unrounded [ʌ] under vowel reduction. The best-known case is Russian akanye: unstressed /o/ after a hard consonant in the syllable immediately before the stress (and word-initially) merges with /a/ as [ʌ], phonetically closer to [ɐ] in standard Moscow speech, so that мороз /moˈroz/ is [mʌˈros]. Further from the stress the same /o/ reduces to [ə].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Unrounding, Vowel Reduction",
  "languageExamples": [
